  "content": {
    "display_title": "Faithfulness of the representation on the CAR trace Hilbert space",
    "one_sentence_role": "Proves that the representation $\\widetilde\\rho:T\\to B(H_\\tau)$ is injective.",
    "natural_language_statement": "Let $A$ be the completed CAR algebra and $T=C^*(\\pi(A)\\cup\\{L_i:i\\in I\\})\\subseteq B(\\mathcal H)$ the algebra generated by the selected direct sum $\\pi$ of pure-state GNS representations indexed by their unitary-equivalence classes $i\\in I$ and the unitaries $L_i$ from the fixed homogeneity and shell construction. Write $\\iota(a)=\\pi(a)$ for the embedding $A\\to T$. Let $\\tau$ be the CAR trace and $\\phi$ its chosen state extension to $T$. Write $\\rho_\\phi:T\\to B(H_\\phi)$ for the GNS representation of $\\phi$, and use the chosen unitary $E:H_\\tau=L^2(A,\\tau)\\to H_\\phi$ from the cited trace construction. For $\\widetilde\\rho(t)=E^{-1}\\rho_\\phi(t)E$, $$\\widetilde\\rho(s)=\\widetilde\\rho(t)\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ s=t\\qquad(s,t\\in T).$$",
    "definition_explanation": null,
    "assumptions": "The automorphisms and unitaries defining $T$ are those fixed by CAR homogeneity. The cited injectivity theorem for the conjugated GNS representation applies to every such shell family, hence to this one.",
    "conclusion": "Thus $\\widetilde\\rho:T\\to B(H_\\tau)$ is faithful. Together with the separately established separability of $H_\\tau$, it realizes this same $T$ faithfully on a separable Hilbert space.",
    "proof_summary": "Cancel the fixed unitary conjugation and use faithfulness of the GNS representation $\\rho_\\phi$ of the state extension.",
    "proof_steps": [
      {
        "step_id": "s1",
        "text": "If $\\widetilde\\rho(s)=\\widetilde\\rho(t)$, then $$\\rho_\\phi(s)=E\\widetilde\\rho(s)E^{-1}=E\\widetilde\\rho(t)E^{-1}=\\rho_\\phi(t).$$ The cited injectivity theorem for $\\rho_\\phi$ gives $s=t$. The Lean proof specializes this same argument to the fixed family."
      }
    ],
    "lean_realization_notes": "This statement concerns injectivity of a representation, not faithfulness of a vector state, and does not assert irreducibility.",
    "content_completeness": "CARD_CONTENT_COMPLETE",
    "language_tag": "en"
  },
